+// across the presets, and boost thresholds up to 4000 rpm late. Nobody matches a turbo that way.
+//
+// A housing is a compromise: tighter spools earlier and costs back-pressure at the top. Every limit
+// on going tighter is now modelled — pumping work (the exhaust half of the loop), residual-gas
+// knock and the timing the ECU pulls for it, residual dilution of VE, and the turbine-temperature
+// fuel cap — so none has to be asserted. The builder's objective is the one the car feels: the
+// most AVERAGE POWER across the engine's own rev range, idle to redline. A lazy low end and a choked
+// top end both lower it; acceleration is power. No tolerance constant (the first attempt used "give
+// up at most 12% of rated power", a judgement call), and no per-type split: a diesel, with no spark
+// to retard, ends up tight because nothing stops it, which is what real turbodiesel housings are.
+//
